{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Take for yourself spices.</b> Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 30:34) by way of the plain sense, that its meaning is: take for yourself spices — they are נָטָף and שְׁחֵלֶת and חֶלְבְּנָה; these are the spices, and pure frankincense with them. And it is not correct to repeat 'spices' another time, for it is not the way of Scripture to repeat what it has mentioned merely by interrupting the matter at length, or in matters that are always so. And the truth is according to the words of our Sages (Keritot 6a), for they are other spices. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 30:34): 'Spices' — two; נָטָף and שְׁחֵלֶת and חֶלְבְּנָה — behold five; 'spices' — to include more like these, behold ten; and frankincense — behold eleven. And one may wonder: why does Scripture not mention them? Perhaps Scripture says: take for yourself spices — נָטָף and שְׁחֵלֶת and חֶלְבְּנָה — many spices, and pure frankincense, and it does not insist except upon these four, for through them the cloud of smoke of the incense rises; only it commanded that he put many spices with them that have a good fragrance, so that the columns of its smoke would be perfumed. And therefore it did not specify the weight or how much to burn from it, for it did not insist except that these four be equal, and that he add with them other good spices for burning (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 3:7).<br>And the reason is that just as it said regarding the anointing oil that he should make it according to the manner of the work of perfumers, and did not teach its procedure but relied upon them, so too it said here, that he should take these four spices in equal weight and add spices to them and make from all of them incense compounded according to the work of perfumers, with the spices known to them that are good together with these, and in the procedure that they follow, and place from it before the Ark of the Testimony so that its smoke rises, as is the custom of incense before kings. And so too it was brief regarding its making (Exodus 38:29), and did not mention it as it mentioned all the others. And so they said in the Gemara (Keritot 6a): Resh Lakish said, what is the meaning of the word קְטֹרֶת? Something that produces smoke and rises, for the Torah commanded in it only things that produce smoke and rise, after the manner of the work of perfumers. And perhaps the selected spices for this were explained orally to Moses at Sinai, along with the entire procedure of the incense, for so too the procedure of the oil was explained to him — how he should make it — even though it depended upon the custom of perfumers. Or perhaps it did not insist at all except upon those explicitly written in Scripture; it commands that he perfume them with other spices according to the way of perfumers. And so they said in Midrash Chazita (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 3:5): the Sages investigated and did not find anything suitable for the incense except these eleven spices alone.<br>And it is further possible that the term סַמִּים and בְּשָׂמִים is one expression, as I have mentioned (Ramban on Exodus 25:6), and these three that it mentioned are not spices, for נָטָף and חֶלְבְּנָה are resins, and שְׁחֵלֶת is a shell that comes from the sea, and Scripture says: take for yourself spices — the ones mentioned above — and נָטָף and שְׁחֵלֶת and חֶלְבְּנָה — and more spices — and pure frankincense, and you shall make incense compounded according to the work of perfumers. For the spices mentioned above (Exodus 30:23) in the oil — מָר דְּרוֹר and קִנָּמוֹן and קִדָּה — enter into the incense, and קְנֵה בֹשֶׂם, in my opinion, is the peel that the Sages mentioned, and it is דְּרַאצִינִי in Arabic, and so too in the language of the Gemara (Shabbat 65a), and it is a cane resembling reeds. And the spices that it commanded to add are שִׁבֹּלֶת נֵרְדְּ and כַּרְכֹּם and קֹשְׁטְ, according to what the Sages taught; and perhaps Scripture insists only upon these nine that it mentioned, but regarding what it doubled to add yet more spices, it does not insist except upon the perfuming, that he make compounded incense. And it was brief regarding its procedure and the weights of the others because it commanded that he make it according to the work of a perfumer. And it appears to me that the Sages chose these three because they are mentioned in Song of Songs (4:14): נֵרְדְּ and כַּרְכֹּם and אֲהָלוֹת, and this is the קֹשְׁטְ, which it calls in the plural because it is of two types, the sweet and the bitter. And behold, there it mentions קָנֶה and קִנָּמוֹן and לְבוֹנָה and מוֹר together with these three, and it says (ibid.) 'with all the chief spices' to include קִדָּה, and behold these are all the spices of the incense. And it first mentions there (ibid. v. 13) 'כְּפָרִים with נְרָדִים' to include נָטָף and שְׁחֵלֶת and חֶלְבְּנָה, which are resins, for כְּפָרִים is from 'וְכָפַרְתָּ אֹתָהּ' (Genesis 6:14) — resin that adheres. And the Jerusalem Targum rendered 'אֲהָלוֹת' (Song of Songs ibid.) — 'אַקְסֵי לַאלּוֹאוָון', and it is the good wood called in Roman tongue linga lovan, for אַקְסֵי is 'wood' in Aramaic, לָאלֹּוָאן as its name, and in Greek it is called exactly אַקְסֵי לוֹאִין, and so too its name in Arabic is אַל עוּרְטִיב. But Onkelos rendered 'כַּאֲהָלִים נָטַע YHWH' (Numbers 24:6) — 'כְּבוּשְׂמַיָּא' — he did not single out for it a specific name." "<b>נָטָף.</b> It is צֳרִי. And it is written in Rashi's commentary: 'and the צֳרִי, they call it טִרְיָגָה, and on account of the fact that it is only a resin dripping from the trees of the balsam, it is called נָטָף.' And I do not know whether this is a scribal error, or whether the one who said it denied it to him, for תִּרְיַאקָה is not a single spice, but it is a compound of many ingredients; leaven and honey and the flesh of creeping things and vermin enter into it, for the powder of scorpions and the flesh of the viper enter into it, for that is why it is so called, for venom in Greek is תְּרִיאַק. And so in the language of the Talmud: טַרְקֵהּ חִוְיָא (Shabbat 109b), and so the compound is mentioned in their language: 'תְּרִיאַקָה לְכוּלֵּיהּ גוּפָא' (Nedarim 41a), and heaven forbid that the flesh of creeping things and vermin and leaven and honey should enter into the incense, and it is written (Leviticus 2:11): 'for any leaven and any honey you shall not burn from it.'<br>But rather, the צֳרִי is the resin, like the manner of oil dripping from the balsam tree, called in the language of the Sages קְטָף, and this is what we learned (Keritot 6a): Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says: the צֳרִי is nothing but resin that comes from the trees of the קְטָף, and perhaps they call it קְטָף because they break its branches in the days of heat and the צֳרִי flows from that dripping. And they said in the Gemara in the chapter 'With what may one light?' (Shabbat 25a): Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says: one may not light with צֳרִי, and so too Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar would say: the צֳרִי is nothing but resin that comes from the trees of the קְטָף, and they explained the reason: because its fragrance permeates — a decree, lest he use it for himself. And behold, it is that esteemed, important oil mentioned.<br>And I wonder, for Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 30:34) translated 'נְכֹאת וּצְרִי וָלֹט' (Genesis 37:25) — 'שְׁעַף וּקְטַף', and likewise 'מְעַט צֳרִי' (ibid. 43:11) — 'קְטַף', and he translated 'נָטָף' — 'נָטוֹפָא', and did not translate נָטָף like צֳרִי. And Jonathan translated 'צֳרִי' in every place as 'שְׁעַף' (thus in Jeremiah 8:22, 46:11, 51:8, and in Ezekiel 27:17; but before us in Genesis he rendered שְׁעַף קְטַף), and it is an expression of anointing oil in the language of the Talmud: 'עָבְדֵי לֵיהּ שִׁיפָא בְּצָעָא' (Chullin 111b), and it is with the letter ע, שְׁעִיפָא, 'דְּשָׁיֵף לֵיהּ מֵהַהוּא מִינָא בְּפֻמֵּהּ' (Beitzah 16a) — דְּשָׁעֵף. And what appears from their opinion is that the צֳרִי tree and its fruit are both called צֳרִי in the sacred language, just as fig and pomegranate and citron and many others are. And נְכֹאת in their view is a name for any esteemed and fragrant oil, and therefore it says 'וַיַּרְאֵם אֶת כָּל בֵּית נְכֹתֹה' (II Kings 20:13), for the storehouse in which that fine oil was kept is called by its name, for it is the choicest of the treasure, and there Scripture writes 'וְאֵת שֶׁמֶן הַטּוֹב', and therefore Onkelos said regarding the gift of Joseph (Genesis 43:11) that they brought him שְׁעַף, which is the name of the anointing oil of this esteemed oil, and they brought from the branches of the tree, which is the צֳרִי called קְטָף; and in other places, when Scripture mentions צֳרִי alone and mentions it for healing and for praise, as in 'the צֳרִי is not in Gilead' (Jeremiah 8:22), Jonathan translates it as the oil, which is שְׁעַף. And Onkelos translated 'נָטָף' — 'נָטוֹפָא', and it is this oil called so because it drips from the breaks of the branches. And there is no place here to translate it שְׁעַף, for it is not in the incense for anointing. And I saw from the Rav, Rabbi Moses (Maimonides, Hilkhot Klei HaMikdash 2:4), that he includes in the incense the wood of the aforementioned tree, called in Arabic 'עוּד בַּלְסַאן'. It appears that he thinks that Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who said that the צֳרִי is nothing but resin dripping from the trees of the קְטָף, comes to disagree and to say that the צֳרִי is not among the spices of the incense, for it is only a resin, and in the incense the resin does not enter it but the קְטָף itself."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The reference to Exodus 38:29 in the printed editions appears to be an error for Exodus 37:29, which is the verse describing the making of the incense; translated the citation as it stands in the source text.", 1 "Ramban quotes Rashi's comment about טִרְיָגָה but the attribution may reflect a secondary gloss in some editions of Rashi rather than Rashi's own words; translated as the text stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:15.813241027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}